//! The technique of each practice in a few exact sentences: posture, hands, words,
//! counts and what each stage really asks of you. Shown first under “The technique”.

pub(super) fn get(id: &str) -> Option<&'static str> {
    Some(match id {
        // ================= Gateway & Lifeline
        "f3" => "<p><b>Focus 3</b> is the introduction: you simply learn what the sound feels like. Breathe slowly, let the tones fill both ears, and notice your mind growing calmer and more even. There is no goal beyond relaxing while staying awake.</p>",
        "f10" => "<p><b>Focus 10, ‘mind awake, body asleep’:</b> after the preparation, let your body become so still and heavy that you lose track of it, while your mind stays clear. To check you're awake, keep noticing the sound, or count slowly from time to time.</p>",
        "f12" => "<p><b>Focus 12, ‘expanded awareness’:</b> from Focus 10, imagine your awareness spreading out beyond your body in every direction, like light filling a large room. Ask one simple question, and notice whatever comes (an image, a word, a feeling) without judging it.</p>",
        "f15" => "<p><b>Focus 15, ‘no time’:</b> let go of past and future, and rest in a dark, still, timeless space. Don't try to see anything; the stillness itself is the experience.</p>",
        "f21" => "<p><b>Focus 21, ‘the bridge’:</b> rest at the edge of awareness where images and impressions arise by themselves. Observe them like a traveller at a border crossing, curious and calm.</p>",
        "f22" => "<p><b>Focus 22</b> is described as the dreamlike edge between waking and sleep. Let loose, dreamy images drift past, and notice them without holding on.</p>",
        "f23" => "<p><b>Focus 23</b> is offered here as quiet reflection: picture a calm, softly lit space and let memories of people you've known come and go with kindness.</p>",
        "f25" => "<p><b>Focus 24 to 26</b> are Monroe's ‘belief system territories’. Treat them as an imaginative exercise: notice the images your own beliefs create, with curiosity and without judgement.</p>",
        "f27" => "<p><b>Focus 27, ‘the Park’:</b> imagine a peaceful park with paths, benches, water and trees. Walk there in your imagination, rest, and enjoy the calm.</p>",

        // ================= Research-based, purpose & sound sessions
        "zen" => "<p>Sit in zazen and count or follow the breath while the beat slows from alpha into theta. Keep the eyes half open to stay alert as the mind settles.</p>",
        "edison" => "<p><b>The Edison (or Dalí) method:</b> sit back in a chair holding something light, such as keys or a spoon, loosely in one hand over the floor. Think about your problem clearly, then let yourself drift. When the object drops and wakes you, write down at once whatever was in your mind. In a 2021 study, people who dozed off briefly like this were far more likely to find a hidden shortcut in a puzzle (Lacaux et al., <i>Science Advances</i>).</p>",
        "lucid" => "<p>As you fall asleep, repeat silently: ‘Next time I'm dreaming, I'll remember that I'm dreaming’, and picture yourself back in a recent dream, noticing something odd and realising it's a dream. This is Stephen LaBerge's MILD technique.</p>",
        "nsdr" => "<p><b>Yoga Nidra:</b> lie on your back, arms a little away from your sides, palms up, a blanket over you. Make a short resolve (<i>sankalpa</i>) in the present tense, such as ‘I am calm and healthy’. Then move your attention around the body as the voice names each part, without moving. Stay aware but don't try to concentrate. Repeat the resolve at the end.</p>",
        "hrv" => "<p>Breathe in for about 5 seconds and out for about 5 seconds, six breaths a minute, softly through the nose and low into the belly. Don't take big breaths; keep them comfortable.</p>",
        "nap" => "<p>Lie down for a short nap. Keep it under 20 to 25 minutes, so you wake from light sleep without grogginess. Set a backup alarm.</p>",
        "anx" => "<p>Let each out-breath be a little longer than the in-breath, and let your shoulders, jaw and belly soften. If anxious thoughts come, label them ‘worry’ and come back to the sound.</p>",
        "monks" => "<p>First bring to mind someone you love easily and let the wish that they be free from suffering grow warm and strong. Then widen it: to everyone, without anyone in particular, so the compassion simply fills the space. This ‘objectless compassion’ is the practice studied in long-term Tibetan meditators (Lutz et al., 2004).</p>",
        "genus" => "<p>Sit comfortably with your eyes open or closed. Research versions of 40 Hz stimulation use about an hour a day of combined light and sound; this session is sound only.</p>",
        "pain" => "<p>Rest your attention near the area of discomfort with curiosity instead of resistance: notice its edges, its texture, how it changes. Breathe softly ‘around’ it, and let the muscles nearby relax.</p>",
        "obe" => "<p><b>The rope technique:</b> once your body feels asleep, imagine a strong rope hanging just above your chest. Reach up with imaginary hands (keep your real body still) and climb, hand over hand. Focus on the feeling of effort and of being pulled; vibrations often follow.</p>",
        "rv" => "<p><b>Remote viewing practice:</b> ask a friend to put a photo in a sealed envelope, or choose one later at random. During ‘Viewing’, note only raw impressions (colours, textures, shapes, sounds, temperatures), not names, and sketch them. Compare with the target afterwards. Treat it as a playful exercise in attention: the US government closed its own programme in 1995 after a review found it had never produced useful intelligence.</p>",
        "shaman" => "<p>Picture an entrance into the earth you know from real life, travel down through it, and explore the landscape you arrive in. Return the same way at the call-back.</p>",
        "pineal" => "<p>Rest your attention lightly between your eyebrows, as if feeling a soft point of light there. Don't roll your eyes up or frown; let the attention, not the eye muscles, do the work.</p>",
        "om" | "omdrone" => "<p>Chant <b>AUM</b> on each slow out-breath: ‘Ahh’ felt in the belly, ‘Ooo’ in the chest, and ‘Mmm’ humming in the head, about equal in length. Then breathe in quietly and listen to the silence before the next one.</p>",
        "vis" => "<p>Picture your goal as already achieved, through your own eyes, with as many senses as you can. Then picture the main obstacle and exactly how you'll handle it. Psychologist Gabriele Oettingen found this ‘mental contrasting’ works better than positive fantasy alone.</p>",
        "wake" => "<p>Sit up, open the curtains if you can, and let your breathing quicken a little. Stretch your arms overhead and roll your shoulders as the beat rises.</p>",
        "study" => "<p>Start your study task once the tone has settled. Keep the volume low and take a five-minute break every 25 to 50 minutes.</p>",
        "create" => "<p>Have your work, an instrument, or a notebook within reach. During the open, flowing stages, let ideas come without judging them; in the shaping stage, choose and develop the best ones.</p>",
        "stress" => "<p>Breathe out slowly through the mouth, as if sighing, and let tension leave with each out-breath: forehead, jaw, shoulders, hands, belly.</p>",
        "insomnia" => "<p>Use it in bed with the lights off, and don't watch the clock. If you're still awake after 20 minutes or so, get up, do something quiet in dim light, and come back when you feel sleepy.</p>",
        "headache" => "<p>Lie down in a dim room. Relax your jaw, forehead, scalp and shoulders one by one; a cool cloth on the forehead can help. See a doctor if headaches are new, severe or come with other symptoms.</p>",
        "euphoria" => "<p>Bring to mind three things you are grateful for, one at a time, and let the warm feeling of each one grow before moving on. Then let the feeling spread through your whole body.</p>",
        "grounding" => "<p>Sit with your bare feet on the floor. With each out-breath, imagine roots growing down from your feet into the earth; with each in-breath, feel steady and supported.</p>",
        "alpha" | "theta" | "delta" | "beta" | "gamma" | "smr" | "alphatheta" | "heal" => return None,
        "hypno" => "<p><b>The classic sequence:</b> stare at one spot until your eyelids feel heavy and let them close; relax the body from head to toe; walk down an imaginary staircase from ten to one, deeper with each step; arrive in a safe place you build with every sense; repeat your suggestion three times; make an anchor by pressing your thumb and finger together; then come up, counting from one to five.</p>",
        "ganzfeld" => "<p><b>Ganzfeld:</b> with an even red light on your closed eyes and steady noise in your ears, the brain starts to fill the blank field with images. Lie back and say aloud, or note to yourself, everything you see, hear or feel, however small or strange.</p>",
        "drumtrance" | "sys_drum" => "<p>Lie or sit still with your eyes covered and let the drum carry you. Let images, movement or memories come without steering them, and come back when the rhythm changes.</p>",
        "tlr" => "<p><b>Targeted Lucidity Reactivation:</b> during the training stages, each time you hear the chime, pause, ask ‘Am I dreaming?’, look at your hands and notice that you are aware. Later in the night the same chime plays softly, to remind you inside a dream. Use it in the second half of the night, after 5 to 6 hours of sleep. Based on Carr et al. (2020) and Konkoly et al. (2021).</p>",
        "swspulses" => "<p>Use it as you go to sleep. The soft pulses are timed to the slow waves of deep sleep; there is nothing to do except lie comfortably.</p>",
        "kirtan" => "<p><b>Kirtan Kriya:</b> chant <i>Saa, Taa, Naa, Maa</i>. On each syllable touch the tip of your thumb to one fingertip, in turn, on both hands: index on Saa, middle on Taa, ring on Naa, little finger on Maa. Imagine each sound flowing in through the top of your head and out between your eyebrows, in an ‘L’ shape. Chant aloud, then in a whisper, then silently (still moving the fingers), then whisper and aloud again. Finish by breathing in deeply, stretching your arms up, and breathing out slowly.</p>",
        "bowls" => "<p>Lie down, cover your eyes, and let the sound wash over you. Notice how each tone shimmers and beats as it fades, and follow it into silence.</p>",
        "mantra6" => "<p>Choose a short prayer or mantra, such as one Hail Mary or <i>Om Mani Padme Hum</i>. Recite it slowly so that one recitation fills one breath of about ten seconds. In a 2001 study, both the rosary in Latin and yoga mantras slowed breathing to almost exactly this rhythm (Bernardi et al., 2001, <i>BMJ</i>).</p>",
        "rain" => "<p>Simply listen. Let the rain cover other sounds, and rest your attention on its changing patterns.</p>",
        "sys_noise" => "<p>Noise only, with no beat: use it to mask distractions while you work or sleep, or as a comparison with the beat sessions.</p>",

        // ================= Astral projection & OBE
        "ap_monroe" => "<p>After the preparation and Focus 10, wait for the <b>vibrational state</b>: a buzzing or rushing through the body. Don't be alarmed; let it spread and grow even. Then, without moving your physical body, imagine rolling over as if turning over in bed, and roll out to the side.</p>",
        "ap_rope" => "<p><b>Robert Bruce's rope technique:</b> imagine a strong rope hanging from the ceiling above you. Reach up with imaginary hands (keep your real body completely still) and climb, hand over hand. Focus on the effort, the pulling, and any dizzy, rising feeling.</p>",
        "ap_muldoon" => "<p>Before you start, choose a familiar target in another room, such as a glass of water in the kitchen. During the session, imagine getting up and walking to it in vivid detail: the floor, the door frames, the feel of the glass.</p>",
        "ap_phase" => "<p><b>Best right after waking from sleep, before you move.</b> Stay completely still with your eyes closed. In the first few seconds, try to roll out or float up without moving. If nothing happens within 3 to 5 seconds, switch to another technique, such as trying to move an imaginary hand or watching the darkness behind your eyelids.</p>",
        "ap_wbtb" => "<p>Wake after 4½ to 6 hours of sleep, stay up for 20 to 30 minutes reading about out-of-body experiences, then lie down with this session and hold the intention to stay aware as your body falls asleep.</p>",
        "ap_theta_gamma" | "ap_bilateral" => "<p>Lie still and let the sound relax your body deeply. Keep a light thread of awareness, and as your body grows heavy, imagine yourself becoming light and free.</p>",
        "ap_float" => "<p>Imagine lying on warm water, perfectly supported. Then feel yourself rising slowly, like a balloon, drifting upwards and gently turning.</p>",
        "ap_hypnotic" => "<p>Follow the hypnotic suggestions as if enjoying a story: heavy eyelids, a relaxed body, a growing lightness, rising, and looking down at the room. Let the imagery be as vivid as it wants to be.</p>",
        "ap_imagined" => "<p>Picture your own body lying where it is now. Then imagine your viewpoint rising to the ceiling and looking around the room from up there, in as much detail as you can, before coming back.</p>",
        "ap_kinesthetic" => "<p>Without moving, imagine the <b>feeling</b> of movement: lifting a little, rotating, rolling, drifting. Focus on sensation rather than pictures. This is based on a woman studied by Smith and Messier (2014) who could do this at will.</p>",
        "ap_cycling" => "<p><b>Michael Raduga's phase cycling:</b> alternate techniques for about 3 to 5 seconds each: <i>phantom wiggling</i> (try to move an imaginary hand), <i>observing images</i> (watch the darkness behind your eyelids), <i>rotation</i> (imagine rolling around your head-to-toe axis) and <i>listening in</i> (listen for inner sounds). Cycle through them until one starts to work, then intensify it.</p>",
        "ap_lab" => "<p>Relax your muscles one group at a time, rest in the red Ganzfeld light and noise, then follow the guided imagery of leaving your body, as in laboratory studies of induced out-of-body experiences.</p>",
        "ap_swing" => "<p>Imagine your body gently rocking, like a swing, back and forth, a little further each time, until it feels as if you could swing free.</p>",
        "ap_christos" => "<p><b>The Christos technique</b> (Glaskin, 1974) was done with helpers rubbing the feet and forehead; here, imagine it. Imagine your legs stretching out through your feet, then your head stretching out through the crown; then expanding like a balloon; then standing at your own front door; then rising above the house, flying somewhere, and landing to explore.</p>",
        "ap_rising" => "<p><b>Rising on the planes</b> (Golden Dawn): imagine yourself rising straight up the Middle Pillar of the Tree of Life, through a veil, towards golden light and then brilliance. Keep a calm, upright attitude, and descend the same way.</p>",
        "ap_tattva" => "<p>Gaze at the tattva symbol on screen for about 20 to 30 seconds, then close your eyes and watch the afterimage. Imagine it growing into a doorway, step through, and explore the landscape you find; then return through the same door. The tattvas are earth (yellow square), water (silver crescent), fire (red triangle), air (blue circle) and spirit (black egg).</p>",
        "ap_phowa" => "<p>Visualise the central channel, a clear tube of light from below the navel to the crown of the head, open at the top. Your awareness is a small bright sphere at the heart; above your head is a presence of pure light and kindness. With each out-breath the sphere rises gently towards it. This gentle version only rehearses the movement; traditional phowa is taught by a lama.</p>",
        "ap_dreamyoga" => "<p>Lie on your right side, right hand under your cheek, in the ‘sleeping lion’ posture. Review the day as if it had been a dream. Rest your attention in a soft red light at the throat, make the intention to recognise your dreams, and let yourself fall asleep.</p>",
        "ap_upper" => "<p>For the <b>upper world</b>, picture yourself climbing a tall tree, a mountain or a ladder, or rising with smoke, until you pass through a thin layer, like a cloud or a membrane, into a landscape above. Ask to meet a teacher or helper there.</p>",
        "ap_middle" => "<p>The <b>middle world</b> is a dreamlike version of this world. Imagine walking out of your door and travelling across the land, noticing everything with fresh eyes.</p>",
        "ap_ssild" => "<p><b>SSILD</b> (Senses-Initiated Lucid Dream): after 4 to 6 hours of sleep, get up for about 5 minutes, then lie back down. Do a few quick cycles of 2 to 3 seconds each: attention on <i>sight</i> (the darkness behind your eyelids), then <i>sound</i>, then <i>body</i> sensations. Then do several slow cycles of about 30 seconds each, and finally just go to sleep. Don't look for results. A 2020 international study found it about as effective as MILD (Aspy).</p>",
        "ap_ground" => "<p>Sit up. Press your feet into the floor and feel your weight in the chair. Open your eyes and name five things you can see, four you can hear and three you can touch. Have a drink of water or a snack.</p>",

        // ================= Spiritual & mystical experiences
        "my_centering" => "<p><b>Centering Prayer:</b> choose a sacred word of one or two syllables (God, Jesus, Abba, Love, Peace) as the symbol of your consent to God's presence. Introduce it silently and gently. Whenever you notice you are engaged with a thought, return ever so gently to the word. At the end, stay in silence with your eyes closed for a couple of minutes. Keating recommended 20 minutes, twice a day.</p>",
        "my_tibetan" => "<p>Visualise a figure (a Buddha, a deity, or simply a radiant light) in front of you, building the details one by one: colour, posture, expression. Hold the whole image steady, then let it merge with you, and rest in absorption.</p>",
        "my_vocal" => "<p>Start by humming; let the hum become sounds without words, then let go of control and let your voice flow freely and joyfully. In Pentecostal churches this is speaking in tongues (glossolalia); here it is simply free vocalisation. Practise somewhere you won't feel self-conscious.</p>",
        "my_jhana" => "<p>Settle your attention on the breath at the nostrils. When attention steadies, turn towards any pleasant feeling (often in the hands, face or chest) and let it fill you. The first jhāna has joy with a little thinking; the second drops the thinking; the third is quiet contentment; the fourth is clear, still equanimity. Treat the stages as an exploration, not a test.</p>",
        "my_nondual" => "<p>Rather than focusing on one thing, let your attention open to everything at once: sounds, sensations, space. Then notice awareness itself, the knowing in which all of it appears, and rest there without dividing experience into ‘inside’ and ‘outside’.</p>",
        "my_inquiry" => "<p><b>Ramana Maharshi's self-inquiry:</b> notice the plain feeling of ‘I’. When a thought arises, ask ‘To whom does this thought come?’ The answer is ‘to me’; then ask ‘Who am I?’ and turn your attention back to the source of the I-feeling. Don't answer with words or ideas; rest in the source.</p>",
        "my_mantra" => "<p>Choose a soft, pleasant sound with no meaning for you. Think it silently, without moving your lips, and let it be as faint and effortless as it likes. When you notice you've drifted into thoughts, come back to the sound without effort. (Transcendental Meditation itself is taught by certified teachers with a personal mantra; this is a generic version.)</p>",
        "my_float" => "<p>Lie in a dark room as still as you can, arms by your sides, and imagine floating in warm, salty water, perfectly supported and weightless. Let the boundaries of your body soften into the darkness.</p>",
        "my_arc" => "<p>Follow the arc the guidance gives: preparation and intention, letting go, then openness to unity, sacredness and timelessness, and finally a slow return and integration. Treat each stage as an invitation, not a demand.</p>",
        "my_dhikr" => "<p>Repeat <i>Lā ilāha illā-llāh</i> (there is no god but God): first aloud, then softly, then silently in the heart, letting the words follow the breath. Finish in silence.</p>",
        "my_jesus" => "<p><b>The Jesus Prayer:</b> as you breathe in, pray ‘Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God’; as you breathe out, ‘have mercy on me, a sinner’. Pray it aloud, then in a whisper, then silently, and let it sink from the lips to the mind and into the heart. If you have a prayer rope (<i>chotki</i>), move one knot per prayer.</p>",
        "my_nada" => "<p><b>Nāda yoga:</b> first listen to the outer sounds around you, then to subtler, quieter ones, then turn inward to the faint inner sounds heard in silence (a high ringing, a hum, a buzz). Follow the subtlest sound you can hear, as the <i>Haṭha Yoga Pradīpikā</i> teaches.</p>",
        "my_vipassana" => "<p><b>Goenka-style vipassana:</b> begin with ānāpāna, observing the breath on the small area below the nostrils. Then move your attention part by part from the top of the head to the toes and back, noticing any sensation (heat, tingling, pressure, or none) with equanimity, without liking or disliking it. End by wishing all beings well (mettā).</p>",
        "my_psychomanteum" => "<p><b>Raymond Moody's psychomanteum:</b> sit in a dim room facing a mirror tilted so that you see only darkness, not yourself, with a dim lamp behind you. Have photos or keepsakes of the person you've lost. Remember them fondly, then gaze softly into the depth of the mirror, without expectation.</p>",
        "my_orbit" => "<p><b>Microcosmic orbit:</b> rest your attention at the lower dantian below the navel. With the tongue on the palate, let attention travel on the in-breath up the spine (tailbone, lower back, between the shoulder blades, back of the head, crown), and on the out-breath down the front (brow, throat, heart, solar plexus, navel). Keep it light; never force the breath.</p>",
        "my_hitbodedut" => "<p><b>Rebbe Nachman's hitbodedut:</b> speak to God in your own words and your own language, aloud but softly, as you would to a close friend: what you're grateful for, what hurts, what you hope for. If no words come, say just that, or repeat one phrase such as ‘Ribbono shel olam’ (Master of the world).</p>",
        "my_guide" => "<p>Imagine a path leading to a peaceful place. Let a figure of wisdom appear: a person, an animal or simply a light. Greet it, ask one question, and accept whatever answer comes, or none. Thank it before you return.</p>",

        // ================= Christian
        "cm_lectio" => "<p><b>Lectio Divina:</b> choose a short passage (5 to 10 verses of a psalm or a Gospel scene). <i>Lectio</i>: read it slowly, aloud, twice. <i>Meditatio</i>: notice a word or phrase that catches you and repeat it, letting it touch your life. <i>Oratio</i>: answer God in your own words. <i>Contemplatio</i>: rest in silence, beyond words.</p>",
        "cm_maranatha" => "<p><b>John Main's Christian Meditation:</b> say the word <i>Maranatha</i> (Aramaic, ‘Come, Lord’) silently as four equal syllables, ma-ra-na-tha, gently and faithfully from beginning to end. Don't think about its meaning; just listen to it. Main recommended 20 to 30 minutes, morning and evening.</p>",
        "cm_ignatian" => "<p><b>Ignatian contemplation:</b> choose a Gospel scene, such as the calming of the storm (Mark 4:35–41). Ask for the grace you desire. Compose the place: see the setting, hear the sounds, smell the air. Enter the scene as yourself or as one of the people there, and let it unfold. End with a colloquy: speak to Jesus as one friend to another.</p>",
        "cm_castle" => "<p><b>Teresa of Ávila's Interior Castle:</b> picture your soul as a castle of clear crystal with many rooms, with God at the centre. Gather your senses inward (recollection), pass through the outer rooms where distractions live, and move inward to the prayer of quiet: a simple, loving rest in God.</p>",
        "cm_cloud" => "<p><b>The Cloud of Unknowing:</b> choose one short word, God or Love. Put every thought beneath a ‘cloud of forgetting’. Then, with a simple longing directed to God, ‘beat’ on the ‘cloud of unknowing’ above you with your word, like a dart of love, again and again.</p>",
        "cm_taize" => "<p>Sing along with the short, repeated chant at a comfortable pitch, letting the repetition calm you. When the singing stops, let the chant carry on silently in your heart.</p>",
        "cm_quaker" => "<p><b>Quaker worship:</b> sit in silence, waiting expectantly for the inner light. Let your thoughts settle (‘centering down’). If something rises in you as a message, hold it quietly; in a meeting, Friends speak only when strongly moved.</p>",
        "cm_vigil" => "<p><b>Unlike most sessions, this one is done lying in bed when you wake in the night.</b> Keep the room dark and the volume low. Pray slowly, a psalm or a few familiar words, or simply rest awake, then let the ‘second sleep’ come.</p>",
        "cm_bestill" => "<p>Breathe the verse ‘Be still, and know that I am God’ (Psalm 46:10), dropping the last word each round: Be still and know that I am… Be still and know… Be still… Be.</p>",
        "cm_visio" => "<p><b>Visio Divina:</b> set an icon or sacred image beside the screen, or use the candle on screen. Gaze softly, let your eyes rest on details, and notice where your attention is drawn. Let yourself be ‘seen’ by the image; respond in prayer; then rest.</p>",
        "cm_rosary" => "<p><b>The Rosary:</b> on the crucifix, pray the Apostles' Creed; on the first large bead, the Our Father; on the next three beads, three Hail Marys; then the Glory Be. For each of the five decades: announce the mystery, pray the Our Father on the large bead and ten Hail Marys on the small beads while picturing the mystery, then the Glory Be. Close with the Hail, Holy Queen. Traditionally the Joyful Mysteries are prayed on Monday and Saturday, the Sorrowful on Tuesday and Friday, the Glorious on Wednesday and Sunday, and the Luminous on Thursday.</p>",
        "cm_lawrence" => "<p><b>Brother Lawrence's way:</b> during an ordinary task (washing up, cooking, walking), speak inwardly to God in simple words, offering each small action and returning to Him whenever you notice you've forgotten. He felt as close to God among the pots and pans as at the altar.</p>",
        "cm_examen" => "<p><b>The Examen</b> of St Ignatius, in five steps: give thanks for the day's gifts; ask for light to see clearly; review the day hour by hour, noticing where you felt close to God or far away; ask forgiveness for what went wrong; and resolve, with God's help, to do better tomorrow.</p>",
        "cm_compline" => "<p><b>Compline</b> is the Church's night prayer: a brief examination of conscience, a psalm (traditionally Psalm 4, 91 or 134), the responsory ‘Into your hands, Lord, I commend my spirit’ (<i>In manus tuas</i>), and a final blessing. Afterwards monks keep the ‘great silence’ until morning.</p>",
        "cm_kyrie" => "<p>Sing or say <i>Kyrie eleison</i> (KEE-ree-eh eh-LAY-ee-sohn, ‘Lord, have mercy’), <i>Christe eleison</i> (KREE-steh, ‘Christ, have mercy’), <i>Kyrie eleison</i>. Let each phrase take one long out-breath.</p>",
        "cm_eckhart" => "<p><b>Gelassenheit</b>, letting go and letting be: release your grip on things, then on your own will, then even on images of God, and rest in what Eckhart called the ‘ground’ of the soul, where God and the soul are one.</p>",

        // ================= Tibetan Buddhism & Bön
        "tb_shamatha" => "<p><b>Three stages, as taught by B. Alan Wallace:</b> mindfulness of breathing (feel the sensations of breathing throughout the body); settling the mind in its natural state (watch thoughts and images arise in the space of the mind without following them); and awareness of awareness (simply rest in being aware, with no object).</p>",
        "tb_tonglen" => "<p><b>Tonglen:</b> breathing in, imagine taking in suffering as hot, heavy, dark smoke; breathing out, send out relief as cool, light, bright light. Start with the textures, then do it for one person you know who is suffering, then for everyone who feels the same. If it feels too heavy, start with yourself.</p>",
        "tb_mani" => "<p>Visualise <b>Chenrezig</b> (Avalokiteśvara), white, with four arms, seated on a lotus and a moon disc above your head or in front of you, with light streaming from his heart to all beings. Recite <i>Om Mani Padme Hum</i> (Tibetan: Om Mani Peme Hung). At the end he dissolves into light, and into you; rest in that.</p>",
        "tb_tara" => "<p>Visualise <b>Green Tara</b>, emerald green, seated with her right leg extended as if ready to step forward to help; her right hand rests on her knee in the gesture of giving, her left at her heart holding a blue lotus. Recite <i>Om Tare Tuttare Ture Soha</i>.</p>",
        "tb_medicine" => "<p>Visualise the <b>Medicine Buddha</b>, deep blue like lapis lazuli, holding a bowl of healing nectar in his left hand and a medicinal plant in his right. Recite <i>Tayata Om Bekandze Bekandze Maha Bekandze Radza Samudgate Soha</i>, and imagine healing blue light filling you and others.</p>",
        "tb_vajra" => "<p><b>Vajra recitation:</b> breathing in, silently OM, with white light at the crown; between breaths, AH, with red light at the throat; breathing out, HUM, with blue light at the heart. Keep the breath natural; there is no need to hold it.</p>",
        "tb_phat" => "<p><b>Patrul Rinpoche's Three Words:</b> relax completely. Then sound a sharp ‘PHAT!’ (inwardly or aloud) that cuts through thinking. In the open gap afterwards, notice the clear awareness that is there before thoughts return; recognise it, decide on it, and gain confidence in it.</p>",
        "tb_tilopa" => "<p><b>Tilopa's six words of advice</b> (Mahāmudrā): don't recall (let go of the past); don't imagine (the future); don't think (about the present); don't examine; don't control; rest. Each stage drops one more kind of effort.</p>",
        "tb_chod" => "<p><b>Feeding your demons</b>, in Lama Tsultrim Allione's five steps: find where you feel the ‘demon’ (a fear, habit or illness) in your body and notice its colour, texture and temperature; let it appear as a figure in front of you and ask what it wants, what it needs, and how it will feel when it gets it; change places and answer as the demon; then return to your place, dissolve your body into a nectar of whatever it needs and feed it until it is satisfied, noticing who or what it becomes; finally, rest in open awareness.</p>",
        "tb_bardo" => "<p>Rehearse the stages of dying described in Tibetan teachings: earth dissolving into water (heaviness), water into fire (dryness), fire into wind (cooling), wind into consciousness; then the white, red and black appearances, and the clear light. The aim is to make sleep and death familiar and peaceful.</p>",
        "tb_gyuto" => "<p>Listen to the deep chord. If you like, hum a very low note along with it, but never strain your voice to reach the overtones; a relaxed hum is enough.</p>",
        "tb_sleepyoga" => "<p><b>Sleep yoga:</b> lie on your right side in the lion posture, right hand under your cheek. Visualise a small, bright light at your heart and let your awareness merge with it as you fall asleep.</p>",
        "tb_sky" => "<p><b>Sky gazing:</b> sit outdoors or at a window facing open sky, with the sun behind you. Gaze into space with relaxed, open eyes, not focusing on anything, and let your mind and the space mix. <b>Never look at the sun.</b></p>",
        "tb_vajrasattva" => "<p><b>Vajrasattva and the four powers:</b> regret (acknowledge harm you have done), reliance (take refuge and wish to help all beings), the remedy (visualise Vajrasattva, white, above your head, and recite his mantra while white nectar flows down through you, washing out negativity like dark liquid) and resolve (commit not to repeat it). The short mantra is <i>Om Benza Satto Hung</i>.</p>",
        "tb_manjushri" => "<p>Visualise <b>Mañjuśrī</b>, golden orange, raising a flaming sword in his right hand (cutting through ignorance) and holding at his heart a lotus that bears a book of wisdom. Recite <i>Om A Ra Pa Tsa Na Dhih</i>, and if you like repeat <i>Dhih</i> many times at the end.</p>",
        "tb_guruyoga" => "<p><b>Guru yoga:</b> visualise Guru Rinpoche (Padmasambhava) in the sky in front of you. Three lights stream from him: white from his forehead to yours (body), red from his throat to yours (speech), blue from his heart to yours (mind). Recite <i>Om Ah Hung Benza Guru Pema Siddhi Hung</i>. Finally he dissolves into light and into you; rest in open awareness.</p>",
        "tb_whitea" => "<p>Sound a long <i>Aaa</i>, three times. Visualise a white Tibetan letter A at your heart, in a sphere of rainbow light; then let it dissolve, and rest as open awareness.</p>",

        // ================= Buddhist
        "bu_metta" => "<p><b>Mettā:</b> silently repeat four phrases, such as ‘May I be safe. May I be happy. May I be healthy. May I live with ease.’ Begin with yourself (or with a benefactor if that is easier), then a friend, a stranger, a mildly difficult person, and finally all beings. Don't force a feeling; the sincere intention is the practice.</p>",
        "bu_brahma" => "<p><b>The four immeasurables:</b> loving-kindness (may all beings be happy), compassion (may they be free from suffering), appreciative joy (may their happiness last) and equanimity (a balanced, steady heart towards all). Radiate each in every direction: in front, to the right, behind, to the left, above and below.</p>",
        "bu_anapana" => "<p><b>The sixteen steps</b> of the Ānāpānasati Sutta, in four groups: the <i>body</i> (long and short breaths, the whole body, calming the body); <i>feelings</i> (joy, happiness, mental activity, calming it); the <i>mind</i> (knowing it, gladdening it, steadying it, releasing it); and <i>insight</i> (impermanence, fading, cessation, letting go).</p>",
        "bu_noting" => "<p><b>Mahāsi noting:</b> note ‘rising’ as the belly rises and ‘falling’ as it falls. When something else takes your attention, note it softly once or twice (‘thinking’, ‘hearing’, ‘itching’) and return. The note is quiet, a small part of attention; most of the attention goes to feeling the experience directly.</p>",
        "bu_mu" => "<p><b>Mu:</b> a monk asked Zhaozhou (Jōshū) whether a dog has Buddha-nature, and he answered ‘Mu’ (‘no’). Breathe out a long, silent ‘Muuu’ into the belly. Don't think about the meaning; become Mu, and let the great doubt build.</p>",
        "bu_shikantaza" => "<p><b>Shikantaza</b> has no object: sit upright in zazen, eyes half open facing a wall, hands in the cosmic mudrā, and just sit. Let thoughts come and go without following or pushing them away; Dōgen called this ‘thinking not-thinking’.</p>",
        "bu_nembutsu" => "<p>Recite <i>Namu Amida Butsu</i> (na-mu a-mi-da bu-tsu, ‘I take refuge in Amida Buddha’) with a relaxed, trusting heart. The setting-sun image comes from the Contemplation Sutra: picture the sun going down in the west, round like a hanging drum.</p>",
        "bu_daimoku" => "<p>Chant <i>Nam-myoho-renge-kyo</i> (nahm myoh-hoh ren-geh kyoh) in a steady, clear rhythm, hands together at your chest, facing a wall or a Gohonzon if you have one. Nichiren Buddhists chant morning and evening.</p>",
        "bu_kasina" => "<p><b>Kasiṇa:</b> gaze at the coloured disc on screen with relaxed eyes from about an arm's length, noting silently ‘blue, blue’. When you can see the image with your eyes closed (the ‘learning sign’), keep it in mind. With long practice a bright, steady image (the ‘counterpart sign’) may appear.</p>",
        "bu_ajikan" => "<p><b>Ajikan</b> (Shingon): gaze at the moon disc on screen, or visualise a white moon disc at your heart, with the Siddham letter A on a lotus inside it. Sound a soft ‘Ah’. Let the disc expand until it fills the universe, then return it to its own size at your heart.</p>",
        "bu_buddho" => "<p>Silently say ‘bud’ on the in-breath and ‘dho’ on the out-breath. When the word fades by itself, let it go and stay with the clear knowing that remains.</p>",
        "bu_maranasati" => "<p>Reflect calmly on the Five Remembrances: I am of the nature to grow old; to become ill; to die; everything dear to me will change and be lost; my actions are my only true belongings. Then ask: ‘Knowing this, how do I want to live today?’</p>",
        "bu_walking" => "<p><b>Walking meditation:</b> choose a straight path of 10 to 20 steps, indoors or out. Walk slowly, hands clasped in front of you or behind your back, eyes lowered a couple of metres ahead. Note ‘left, right’, then in three parts, ‘lifting, moving, placing’. At the end of the path stop, note ‘standing’, turn slowly (‘turning’) and walk back. Keep the phone in a pocket.</p>",
        "bu_buddhanussati" => "<p>Chant the homage <i>Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sammāsambuddhassa</i> three times (homage to the Blessed One, the Worthy One, the Perfectly Self-Awakened One), then the Itipiso verse of the Buddha's qualities. Then reflect quietly on one quality, such as ‘perfectly awakened’ or ‘kind teacher’.</p>",
        "bu_susokukan" => "<p>Count each out-breath silently, from ‘one’ to ‘ten’, then begin again at one. If you lose count or find yourself at fourteen, simply start again at one, without judging.</p>",
        "bu_huatou" => "<p>Ask ‘Who is reciting the Buddha's name?’, or simply ‘Who?’ Don't answer with thoughts. Keep the question alive as a felt sense of not-knowing, the ‘doubt sensation’, as steadily as a cat watching a mouse hole.</p>",
        "bu_whatisthis" => "<p>Ask ‘What is this?’ with real wonder about what is hearing, seeing and knowing right now. Stay with the ‘don't know’ that comes, and ask again when it fades.</p>",
        "bu_silentillum" => "<p><b>Silent illumination:</b> sit upright and be aware of your whole body sitting. Then open to the whole environment, the sounds, light and space, without picking anything out. <i>Silence</i> is no commentary; <i>illumination</i> is clear awareness; let both be present together.</p>",
        "bu_heartsutra" => "<p>Chant <i>Gate gate pāragate pārasaṃgate bodhi svāhā</i> (ga-teh ga-teh pa-ra-ga-teh pa-ra-sam-ga-teh bo-dhi swa-ha: ‘gone, gone, gone beyond, gone completely beyond, awakening, so be it’) in time with the wooden fish. Then sit in silence with the openness the sutra points to.</p>",
        "bu_softbutter" => "<p><b>Hakuin's soft butter:</b> imagine a lump of soft, fragrant butter the size of a duck's egg resting on the crown of your head. As it warms it melts and flows down over your head, shoulders, chest, inner organs, belly and legs to the soles of your feet, washing away tension and pain, and pools around you like warm water.</p>",
        "bu_amituofo" => "<p>Recite <i>Namo Amituofo</i> (nah-moh ah-mee-twoh-foh) in time with the wooden fish, then just <i>Amituofo</i>. Master Yinguang advised reciting clearly with the mouth and hearing each syllable clearly with the ears.</p>",
        "bu_guanyin" => "<p>Recite <i>Namo Guanshiyin Pusa</i> (nah-moh gwahn-shr-yin poo-sah), calling on the bodhisattva who hears the cries of the world. Then, as the Śūraṅgama Sutra describes, turn your hearing inward and listen to the hearing itself.</p>",

        // ================= Hindu & yogic
        "hy_trataka" => "<p><b>Trāṭaka:</b> place a candle at arm's length with the flame at eye level, in a room without draughts, or use the point on screen. Gaze at it without blinking for as long as is comfortable, never straining. Then close your eyes and watch the afterimage, keeping it centred until it fades. Repeat.</p>",
        "hy_soham" => "<p><b>So'ham:</b> listen to the natural breath. The in-breath sounds ‘So’, the out-breath ‘Ham’ (‘hum’). Don't control the breath; just hear the mantra in it, and notice the small pauses between breaths.</p>",
        "hy_vbt" => "<p>Five of the <i>Vijñāna Bhairava Tantra</i>'s doorways: rest in the still point where the breath turns; follow a sound as it fades into silence; feel the body as empty space; contemplate the open sky; and notice the moment between waking and sleep.</p>",
        "hy_bija" => "<p>Chant each seed sound at its centre: <i>LAM</i> at the base of the spine, <i>VAM</i> below the navel, <i>RAM</i> at the solar plexus, <i>YAM</i> at the heart, <i>HAM</i> at the throat, <i>OM</i> between the eyebrows, and silence at the crown. Let the final ‘m’ hum, and feel it vibrate in each place.</p>",
        "hy_shiva" => "<p>Recite <i>Om Namaḥ Śivāya</i> (om na-mah shi-vaa-ya), the five-syllable mantra, one bead of the mālā per repetition; 108 beads make a round.</p>",
        "hy_gayatri" => "<p><b>Sandhyā</b> means ‘junction’: dawn or dusk. Face the rising or setting sun, or the east. Recite: <i>Om bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ / tat savitur vareṇyam / bhargo devasya dhīmahi / dhiyo yo naḥ pracodayāt</i>: ‘We meditate on the glorious light of the divine Sun; may it inspire our minds.’</p>",
        "hy_mahamantra" => "<p><i>Hare Kṛṣṇa Hare Kṛṣṇa, Kṛṣṇa Kṛṣṇa Hare Hare / Hare Rāma Hare Rāma, Rāma Rāma Hare Hare</i>. In <b>japa</b>, chant it softly on the beads, one mantra per bead; in <b>kīrtan</b>, sing it joyfully with the group, call and response.</p>",
        "hy_antarmouna" => "<p><b>Antar Mouna</b> (Satyananda) in stages: awareness of outer sounds; watching spontaneous thoughts without getting involved; choosing a thought, thinking it through and putting it aside; watching thoughts and letting them go; and finally resting in thoughtlessness.</p>",
        "hy_mandukya" => "<p>Chant <b>AUM</b> slowly: ‘A’ (ah) stands for waking; ‘U’ (oo) for dreaming; ‘M’ (mmm) for deep sleep; and the silence afterwards for <i>turīya</i>, ‘the fourth’, pure awareness. Rest in that silence.</p>",
        "hy_netineti" => "<p><b>Neti neti</b> (‘not this, not this’): notice the body and see that you are aware of it, so you are not only the body. Do the same with sensations, emotions and thoughts. What remains is the witnessing awareness. Don't reject experience; just stop identifying with it.</p>",
        "hy_japa" => "<p>Hold the mālā in your right hand over the middle finger, and with each <i>Om</i> draw one bead towards you with the thumb; don't use the index finger and don't cross the guru bead. Chant aloud (<i>vācika</i>), then in a whisper (<i>upāṃśu</i>), then silently (<i>mānasa</i>), which the tradition considers the most powerful.</p>",
        "hy_samyama" => "<p>Choose a simple object (a flame, the breath, a word). <i>Dhāraṇā</i>: hold attention on it, returning whenever it wanders. <i>Dhyāna</i>: attention flows to it unbroken. <i>Samādhi</i>: only the object shines, as if you had disappeared. Patañjali calls the three together <i>saṃyama</i>.</p>",
        "hy_pratyahara" => "<p>Listen to the most distant sounds, then nearer ones, then sounds in the room, then the body (its contact with the floor, the breath), and finally rest in the dark space behind the forehead (<i>chidākāśa</i>).</p>",
        "hy_sriyantra" => "<p>Gaze at a picture of the <b>Śrī Yantra</b>, or imagine it: the outer square with four gates, the circles of lotus petals, the interlocking triangles (four pointing up for Śiva, five pointing down for Śakti), and the central point, the <i>bindu</i>. Move your attention inward, layer by layer, and rest at the bindu.</p>",

        // ================= Kundalini (gentle)
        "kn_adi" => "<p>Sit with a straight spine and press your palms together at the centre of your chest, thumbs against the breastbone. Breathe in deeply and chant <i>Ong Namo</i> (ong nah-mo), take a quick sip of breath, then chant <i>Guru Dev Namo</i> (goo-roo dayv nah-mo). <i>Ong</i> resonates in the nose and the back of the throat. Three times is the usual way to tune in.</p>",
        "kn_satnam" => "<p>Chant a long <i>Saaat</i> (rhymes with ‘but’) and a short <i>Naam</i>, focusing at the brow point. Then breathe it silently: <i>Sat</i> as you breathe in, <i>Naam</i> as you breathe out.</p>",
        "kn_ramadasa" => "<p><b>Ra Ma Da Sa:</b> tuck your elbows against your ribs, angle your forearms out and up, and turn your palms flat and facing up. Chant one full round on each breath: <i>Ra Ma Da Sa, Sa Say So Hung</i> (rah mah dah sah, sah say soh hung). Pull the navel in slightly on <i>Hung</i>. Many teachers end with a breath hold; this gentle version simply breathes while picturing someone who needs healing, held in warm light.</p>",
        "kn_gong" => "<p><b>Lie on your back</b>, arms by your sides, palms up, with a blanket over you (the body cools quickly in deep relaxation). Let the gong's waves wash through you without analysing them.</p>",
        "kn_serpent" => "<p>Sit upright and breathe slowly and naturally. Imagine a soft light at the base of your spine. With each in-breath it rises a little along the spine through the chakras towards the crown; with each out-breath it settles. Keep it gentle; nothing is forced.</p>",

        // ================= Jain & Sikh
        "jn_navkar" => "<p><b>The Navkār Mantra:</b> <i>Namo Arihantāṇaṃ</i> (I bow to the enlightened ones), <i>Namo Siddhāṇaṃ</i> (to the liberated souls), <i>Namo Āyariyāṇaṃ</i> (to the spiritual leaders), <i>Namo Uvajjhāyāṇaṃ</i> (to the teachers), <i>Namo Loe Savva Sāhūṇaṃ</i> (to all monks and nuns in the world). It honours qualities rather than any particular person.</p>",
        "jn_preksha" => "<p><b>Prekṣā dhyāna:</b> first relax completely (<i>kāyotsarga</i>). Then perceive the breath, long and slow; then perceive the body part by part, exactly as it is, without liking or disliking: perceive, don't react.</p>",
        "jn_samayika" => "<p><b>Sāmāyika:</b> begin with the vow, ‘For forty-eight minutes I will stay in equanimity and avoid all harmful activity.’ Sit still; recite the Navkār; reflect, read scripture or meditate. Close by asking forgiveness of all beings: <i>Khāmemi savve jīvā</i> (I forgive all living beings).</p>",
        "jn_kayotsarga" => "<p><b>Kāyotsarga:</b> stand with your feet a little apart and your arms hanging loosely, as in the statues of the tīrthaṅkaras, or lie down. Stay completely still and repeat silently, ‘I am not this body; I am the soul,’ while relaxing each part.</p>",
        "sk_waheguru" => "<p>Sing <i>Waheguru</i> (vaa-hay-goo-roo, ‘Wondrous Lord’) with the kirtan, then breathe it silently: <i>Vaahe</i> on the in-breath, <i>Guru</i> on the out-breath.</p>",
        "sk_mulmantar" => "<p><b>The Mūl Mantar:</b> <i>Ik Onkār</i> (One Creator), <i>Sat Nām</i> (Truth is the Name), <i>Kartā Purakh</i> (the Creator), <i>Nirbhau</i> (without fear), <i>Nirvair</i> (without hate), <i>Akāl Mūrat</i> (timeless form), <i>Ajūnī</i> (beyond birth), <i>Saibhaṅ</i> (self-existent), <i>Gur Prasād</i> (known by the Guru's grace). Then reflect on one quality, such as fearlessness.</p>",

        // ================= Sufi
        "su_sema" => "<p><b>Seated sema:</b> sit on your heels or cross-legged. Cross your arms over your chest with your hands on your shoulders, the Mevlevi gesture of unity, and bow. Unfold the arms: right palm turned up to receive from heaven, left palm turned down to give to the earth. Turn gently from the waist, slowly from side to side or in small circles, never to the point of dizziness, repeating <i>Allāh</i> silently.</p>",
        "su_muraqaba" => "<p><b>Murāqaba:</b> sit with your eyes closed and your attention on the heart (in Naqshbandi teaching, two finger-widths below the left nipple), waiting for God's grace as attentively and silently as a cat at a mouse hole.</p>",
        "su_lataif" => "<p><b>The five subtle centres</b> of the Mujaddidi path: <i>qalb</i> (heart), two finger-widths below the left nipple; <i>rūḥ</i> (spirit), the same place on the right; <i>sirr</i> (secret), two finger-widths above the left nipple; <i>khafī</i> (hidden), the same on the right; <i>akhfā</i> (most hidden), the centre of the chest. Rest your attention in each, feeling <i>Allāh</i> there.</p>",
        "su_breaths" => "<p><b>Hazrat Inayat Khan's purification breaths</b>, five breaths each: <i>earth</i>, in and out through the nose; <i>water</i>, in through the nose, out through the mouth; <i>fire</i>, in through the mouth, out through the nose; <i>air</i>, in and out through the mouth; <i>ether</i>, a fine, almost imperceptible breath through the nose. Keep every breath gentle.</p>",
        "su_names" => "<p><b>Wazīfa:</b> repeat each Divine Name slowly, feeling its quality: <i>Yā Laṭīf</i> (O Subtle, Gentle One), <i>Yā Raḥmān</i> (O Compassionate), <i>Yā Nūr</i> (O Light), <i>Yā Wadūd</i> (O Loving), and finally <i>Hū</i>.</p>",
        "su_jahri" => "<p>Chant <i>Lā ilāha illā-llāh</i> (laa i-laa-ha il-lal-laah) with the drum. On <i>Lā ilāha</i> turn the head gently to the right; on <i>illā-llāh</i> bring the chin down towards the heart, on the left. Keep the movements small and soft.</p>",
        "su_hu" => "<p>Chant <i>Allāh Hū</i>: <i>Allāh</i> rising, <i>Hū</i> breathed out softly into the heart. Then let it become only <i>Hū</i>, and finally just the breath.</p>",
        "su_tasbih" => "<p><b>Tasbīḥ:</b> say <i>Subḥānallāh</i> (glory be to God) 33 times, <i>Al-ḥamdu lillāh</i> (praise be to God) 33 times and <i>Allāhu akbar</i> (God is greatest) 33 times (or 34), counting on the finger joints of the right hand or on beads.</p>",
        "su_ney" => "<p><b>Samāʿ</b>, spiritual listening: listen to the ney as a voice of longing. Rūmī's <i>Masnavī</i> opens with the reed flute lamenting that it was cut from its reed bed; let it remind you of your own longing for your source.</p>",

        // ================= Jewish
        "jk_shema" => "<p>Cover your eyes with your right hand, as is customary, and recite <i>Shema Yisrael, Adonai Eloheinu, Adonai Echad</i> (Hear, O Israel: the Lord is our God, the Lord is One). Lengthen the final ‘d’ of <i>Echad</i> and contemplate God's oneness filling the four directions, above and below. Then softly: <i>Barukh shem kevod malkhuto le-olam va-ed</i>; then <i>Ve-ahavta</i>, ‘you shall love’.</p>",
        "jk_abulafia" => "<p><b>Abulafia's letter chanting:</b> on long, slow out-breaths, sound the letter Alef with each vowel in turn (oh, ah, eh, ee, oo), resting one breath between sounds; Abulafia also taught gentle head movements following each vowel. Keep the pace slow and let the combinations absorb the mind. Then see the letter Alef shining in your mind.</p>",
        "jk_tree" => "<p>Picture the ten sefirot in your body: <i>Keter</i> above the head; <i>Ḥokhmah</i> and <i>Binah</i> at the right and left of the head; <i>Ḥesed</i> and <i>Gevurah</i> at the right and left shoulders; <i>Tiferet</i> at the heart; <i>Netzaḥ</i> and <i>Hod</i> at the right and left hips; <i>Yesod</i> at the base of the body; <i>Malkhut</i> at the feet. Draw light down from above through each in turn.</p>",
        "jk_ayin" => "<p><b>Bittul</b>, self-nullification: notice the sense of being ‘something’ (<i>yesh</i>), a separate self. Let it soften into ‘nothing’ (<i>ayin</i>), the divine source from which everything is renewed at every moment.</p>",
        "jk_merkavah" => "<p>Imagine ascending through seven palaces (<i>heikhalot</i>), each brighter than the last, while the hymn of praise, <i>Kadosh, kadosh, kadosh</i> (Holy, holy, holy), carries you up to the Divine Throne. Rest there in awe, then descend gently, palace by palace.</p>",
        "jk_niggun" => "<p>Hum, then sing, the wordless tune on syllables such as ‘yai-dai-dai’ or ‘bim-bam’, many times over. Let it carry you into <i>devekut</i>, cleaving to God, and then into silence.</p>",
        "jk_hitbonenut" => "<p><b>Hitbonenut:</b> take one idea, here ‘there is nothing else besides Him’ (Deuteronomy 4:35), and think it through in detail and with examples until it shapes how you feel. In Chabad teaching, understanding should become feeling, and feeling should become action.</p>",
        "jk_cheshbon" => "<p><b>Cheshbon ha-nefesh:</b> choose one trait (<i>middah</i>) for the week, such as patience, order or humility. Each evening, review the day: when did I live this trait well, and when not? Write one line in a notebook, honestly and without self-punishment.</p>",
        "jk_omer" => "<p>Meditate on the seven lower sefirot as qualities of the heart: <i>Ḥesed</i> (loving-kindness), <i>Gevurah</i> (strength and restraint), <i>Tiferet</i> (harmony and compassion), <i>Netzaḥ</i> (endurance), <i>Hod</i> (humility and gratitude), <i>Yesod</i> (bonding), <i>Malkhut</i> (presence). Ask how each lives in you, and where it is out of balance.</p>",

        // ================= Taoist
        "ta_zuowang" => "<p><b>Zuowang</b>, ‘sitting and forgetting’, from the <i>Zhuangzi</i>: let go of the body, then of the senses, then of knowing, until you merge with the great Way.</p>",
        "ta_xinzhai" => "<p><b>Xinzhai</b>, ‘fasting of the heart-mind’: as Confucius advises in the <i>Zhuangzi</i>, don't listen with the ears but with the heart-mind; then not with the heart-mind but with the <i>qi</i>, which is empty and waits for all things.</p>",
        "ta_standing" => "<p><b>Zhan zhuang:</b> stand with your feet parallel, shoulder-width apart, knees soft. In <i>wuji</i> the arms hang loosely. In ‘holding the tree’, raise your arms in a circle in front of your chest as if hugging a large tree, palms facing you, elbows a little lower than the wrists, shoulders down. Breathe naturally. Start with 2 to 5 minutes in the posture, and lower your arms whenever you need to.</p>",
        "ta_sixsounds" => "<p><b>Liu Zi Jue</b>, the six healing sounds: <i>Xū</i> (shoo) for the liver, <i>Hē</i> (huh) for the heart, <i>Hū</i> (hoo) for the spleen, <i>Sī</i> (sss) for the lungs, <i>Chuī</i> (chway) for the kidneys and <i>Xī</i> (shee) for the ‘triple burner’. Breathe in through the nose; breathe each sound out softly, almost silently, on a long out-breath.</p>",
        "ta_goldenflower" => "<p><b>Circulating the light:</b> rest your attention lightly between the eyes, at the root of the nose, and ‘turn the light around’: let attention look back towards its own source instead of out at things. Keep the breath so soft you can't hear it.</p>",
        "ta_innersmile" => "<p><b>The Inner Smile:</b> smile gently and feel the smile in your eyes. Send it down to your heart, lungs, liver, kidneys and spleen, then down the digestive tract, then along the spine, thanking each part.</p>",
        "ta_embryonic" => "<p><b>Embryonic breathing:</b> breathe so softly into the lower belly that a feather held under your nose wouldn't move, as if breathing like a baby in the womb. Never hold the breath.</p>",
        "ta_baduanjin" => "<p><b>The eight pieces of brocade</b>, each done slowly 6 to 8 times: (1) two hands hold up the heavens; (2) drawing the bow to shoot the hawk; (3) separating heaven and earth, one hand up and one down; (4) the wise owl looks back; (5) swaying the head and the hips in a low stance; (6) two hands reach down towards the feet; (7) clenching the fists with fierce eyes, punching slowly; (8) rising on the toes and gently dropping onto the heels.</p>",
        "ta_shouyi" => "<p><b>Guarding the One:</b> chant the opening of the <i>Qingjing Jing</i> (the Classic of Purity and Stillness) with the wooden fish. Then rest your attention lightly at the lower dantian, coming back there whenever it wanders.</p>",

        // ================= Shamanic
        "sr_lower" => "<p>Go into an opening in the earth that you know, follow the tunnel down until you come out into a landscape, and ask to meet a power animal. If an animal shows itself to you four times, or from four sides, ask: ‘Are you my power animal?’ Spend time with it, and thank it before you return.</p>",
        "sr_posture" => "<p><b>Felicitas Goodman's ritual postures:</b> in the Bear Spirit posture, stand (or sit) with knees slightly bent, rest your hands on your belly near the navel with the fingertips or knuckles lightly touching, and tilt your head slightly back, eyes closed. Hold it, relaxed and still, for the fifteen minutes of rattling.</p>",
        "sr_dance" => "<p>Clear a safe space with nothing to trip over or bump into. Move freely with the drum, eyes closed or half open, building with the pulse and unwinding as it slows. Finish lying still.</p>",
        "sr_ancestors" => "<p>If you like, set up a small altar first: photos, flowers, a candle in a safe holder, a glass of water or a little food. Say your ancestors' names, tell them what you are grateful for, and ask for their blessing on your life.</p>",
        "sr_fire" => "<p>Write what you want to release on a slip of paper. Offer it to the flame safely (in a fireproof bowl or a candle flame, away from anything flammable), or simply imagine it burning. Then name what you want to grow, and feel it as the warmth of the fire.</p>",

        // ================= Western esoteric & classical
        "we_incubation" => "<p>Choose a question or theme before sleep, as people did in the temples of Asclepius. In this sleep-lab version (modelled on MIT's Dormio studies), you drift towards sleep, a soft prompt brings you back, you say aloud or jot down what was in your mind, and then drift again.</p>",
        "we_plotinus" => "<p><b>Plotinus' ascent:</b> imagine the whole universe as a transparent sphere in which everything can be seen at once; then take away its mass, its place and its matter; then imagine pure intellect, knowing everything at once; and finally ‘take away everything’ (<i>aphele panta</i>) and rest in the One.</p>",
        "we_above" => "<p>‘As above, so below’: feel your body as a small cosmos, then, as the Hermetic text <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i> XI suggests, imagine expanding until you contain the whole universe, all places and all times, and know yourself as one with it.</p>",
        "we_middlepillar" => "<p><b>The Middle Pillar:</b> visualise a sphere of white light above your head and vibrate <i>Eheieh</i> (eh-heh-yeh); at your throat, <i>YHVH Elohim</i> (yeh-ho-vah eh-lo-heem); at your heart, <i>YHVH Eloah ve-Da'ath</i>; at your groin, <i>Shaddai El Chai</i>; at your feet, <i>Adonai ha-Aretz</i>. Join the spheres with a shaft of light, then circulate the light around your body.</p>",
        "we_pentagram" => "<p><b>The Lesser Banishing Ritual of the Pentagram.</b> <i>Qabalistic Cross</i>: touch your forehead, <i>Ateh</i> (unto thee); your breast, <i>Malkuth</i> (the kingdom); your right shoulder, <i>ve-Geburah</i> (and the power); your left shoulder, <i>ve-Gedulah</i> (and the glory); clasp your hands at your chest, <i>le-Olam, Amen</i> (for ever, amen). <i>Pentagrams</i>: facing east, draw a banishing pentagram of earth (start at the lower left point and go up to the top) and vibrate <i>YHVH</i>; turn south, <i>Adonai</i>; west, <i>Eheieh</i>; north, <i>AGLA</i>. <i>Archangels</i>: before me Raphael, behind me Gabriel, on my right Michael, on my left Uriel. Repeat the Qabalistic Cross.</p>",
        "we_bards" => "<p><b>Lie down in complete darkness</b>, as the Gaelic bards did, with something comfortably heavy (a cushion or a book) on your belly. Choose a subject, then let words, images and rhythms rise. Shape lines in your memory without writing them, and write them down when you come back to the light.</p>",
        "we_galdr" => "<p>Chant each rune name long and resonant on one breath: <i>Ansuz</i> (ahn-sooz) for inspiration, <i>Laguz</i> (lah-gooz) for flow, <i>Isa</i> (ee-sah) for stillness, <i>Dagaz</i> (dah-gahz) for breakthrough. Some practitioners also stand in the shape of each rune.</p>",
        "we_viewabove" => "<p>Imagine rising slowly above where you are sitting: above the room, the building, your town, the country, the planet, until human affairs look small and passing. Then come back down and look at a current problem from that height.</p>",
        "we_evening" => "<p>Ask yourself Seneca's questions: What bad habit did I cure today? What fault did I resist? In what way am I better? And the Pythagorean ones: What did I do wrong? What did I do right? What did I leave undone?</p>",
        "we_premeditatio" => "<p><b>Premeditatio malorum:</b> picture a realistic setback, such as a missed train or harsh criticism. Notice the judgement you add (‘this is terrible’). Remind yourself what is in your control, and rehearse your best response, calmly and in detail.</p>",
        "we_gurdjieff" => "<p><b>Self-remembering:</b> divide your attention: sense your right arm, then your right leg, left leg and left arm (Gurdjieff's ‘sensing’ exercise), while at the same time being aware of yourself sensing. Say ‘I am’ silently with the sensation.</p>",
        "we_resh" => "<p><b>Liber Resh:</b> greet the sun four times a day, facing it: at dawn, facing east, as Ra rising; at noon, facing south, as Hathor (Ahathoor); at sunset, facing west, as Tum (Atum); at midnight, facing north, as Khephra. Make a short salute in your own words, then pause in silence.</p>",
        "we_awen" => "<p>Breathe in deeply, then chant <i>Awen</i> (ah-oo-en) on one long out-breath, three times, as Druids do at the start or the end of a ritual. Let the sound open the way to inspiration.</p>",
        "we_pathwork" => "<p><b>Pathworking:</b> imagine standing in the temple of Malkuth, a hall with a black-and-white floor. Go through the door of the 32nd path (the Tau path, linked to Saturn and the World tarot card) and walk the dim path to the violet, moonlit temple of Yesod. Observe what you meet, then return the same way and close the door behind you.</p>",

        // ================= Rosicrucian & alchemical
        "ro_rosecross" => "<p><b>Steiner's rose cross:</b> first think of a plant, growing purely and without passion, and of human blood, which carries our desires; imagine desire purified like the red sap of the rose. Then build the image: a black cross (what is overcome) with seven red roses in a circle around the crossing (purified life). Hold the image and the feelings together.</p>",
        "ro_retrospection" => "<p><b>Max Heindel's retrospection:</b> lying in bed at night, review the day backwards, from evening to morning, like a film in reverse, as an impartial observer. Feel real sorrow for wrongs and real gratitude for the good.</p>",
        "ro_concentration" => "<p>On waking, choose one high ideal, such as love, truth or patience. Hold it in mind without wandering for a few minutes, then meditate on all its aspects, then let it rest and observe what arises.</p>",
        "ro_seed" => "<p>Place a seed in front of you, or imagine one. Think: this seed holds the whole future plant. Picture its growth, root, stem, leaves and flower; then picture the plant withering. Feel the forces of growing and of decaying.</p>",
        "ro_vitriol" => "<p>Descend in imagination into a cave, deep inside the earth, which is deep inside yourself. Meet what you find there honestly, ‘rectify’ it (set it right), and find the hidden stone: your truest self.</p>",
        "ro_chymical" => "<p>Treat Andreae's seven days as an imaginative journey: the invitation, the journey to the castle, the weighing of the guests, the royal wedding, the death and the resurrection of the royal couple. Let each day's image speak to your own life.</p>",
        "ro_stages" => "<p>Work through the colours of the Great Work: <i>nigredo</i>, blackening (face what is dark or stuck); <i>albedo</i>, whitening (purify and clarify); <i>citrinitas</i>, yellowing (the dawn of insight); <i>rubedo</i>, reddening (integration, the ‘stone’).</p>",
        "ro_rosecrossritual" => "<p><b>The Rose Cross Ritual</b> (Golden Dawn): with a stick of incense, or a finger, trace a cross and a circle at each corner of the room (south-east, south-west, north-west, north-east), vibrating <i>Yeheshuah</i> (yeh-hesh-oo-ah) at each, and join them with a line; then trace the cross above and below. End standing in the centre, picturing a rose cross shining on your chest.</p>",

        // ================= Masonic
        "ma_ashlar" => "<p>Picture the <b>rough ashlar</b> (an unshaped stone: your habits and faults) and the <b>perfect ashlar</b> (a squared stone, true and ready for building). Pick one habit and imagine using the common gavel to knock off one rough edge this week.</p>",
        "ma_point" => "<p>Picture a circle with a point at its centre, between two upright parallel lines, with the Volume of the Sacred Law resting on top. The point is you; the circle is the boundary of your conduct; the lines are traditionally St John the Baptist and St John the Evangelist, models of zeal and of learning.</p>",
        "ma_gauge" => "<p><b>The twenty-four-inch gauge:</b> divide the day's 24 hours into three parts: eight for service to God and to those in need, eight for your work, and eight for rest and sleep. Review yesterday by these three parts, then plan tomorrow.</p>",
        "ma_stairs" => "<p>Climb the winding stairs in imagination: three steps (the three degrees), five (the five orders of architecture and the five senses), and seven (the liberal arts: grammar, rhetoric, logic, arithmetic, geometry, music and astronomy). Pause on each to reflect.</p>",

        // ================= Zoroastrian, Bahá'í & Shinto
        "zo_ashem" => "<p>Facing the light, recite <i>Ashem vohū vahištəm astī, uštā astī, uštā ahmāi, hyat ašāi vahištāi ašəm</i>: ‘Righteousness is the best good; it is happiness; happiness to the one who is righteous for the sake of the best righteousness.’ Then reflect on good thoughts, good words and good deeds.</p>",
        "ba_abha" => "<p>Repeat <i>Alláh-u-Abhá</i> (al-LAH-oo-ab-HAH, ‘God is Most Glorious’) ninety-five times, after washing your hands and face and, if possible, sitting facing the Qiblih, as Bahá'u'lláh ordained in the <i>Kitáb-i-Aqdas</i>.</p>",
        "sh_harae" => "<p>If you can, rinse your hands and mouth first. Recite <i>harae tamae, kiyome tamae</i> (ha-ra-eh ta-ma-eh, ki-yo-meh ta-ma-eh: ‘purify, cleanse’) while imagining a clear mountain stream washing through you from head to foot.</p>",
        "sh_chinkon" => "<p>Kneel in seiza, or sit on a chair, hands folded in front of the lower belly. Settle your spirit (<i>tama</i>) at the body's centre, just below the navel, and let the breath and the mind grow still.</p>",

        // ================= Trance & hypnosis
        "tr_autogenic" => "<p><b>J. H. Schultz's six standard exercises</b>, each with a silent formula repeated slowly: heaviness (‘My right arm is heavy’), warmth (‘My right arm is warm’), heartbeat (‘My heartbeat is calm and regular’), breathing (‘It breathes me’), solar plexus (‘My solar plexus is warm’) and a cool forehead (‘My forehead is pleasantly cool’). Don't try to make the sensations happen; let the formula sink in passively. Finish by ‘taking back’: clench your fists, bend your arms, breathe deeply and open your eyes. If you have a heart condition, leave out the heartbeat formula.</p>",
        "tr_erickson" => "<p><b>Betty Erickson's 3-2-1 method:</b> with your eyes open, notice three things you can see, three you can hear and three you can feel; then two of each; then one of each. Close your eyes and repeat the count with inner pictures, sounds and feelings. Then drift into trance with the suggestion you chose before you began.</p>",
        "tr_braid" => "<p><b>James Braid's eye fixation</b> (1843): look at the point on screen, set a little above eye level so that your eyes look slightly upwards. Keep gazing steadily until your eyelids feel heavy and want to close; let them close, and go deeper with each breath.</p>",
        "tr_fractionation" => "<p><b>Oskar Vogt's fractionation:</b> relax deeply, then come up and open your eyes for a moment, then go down again, deeper each time. Keep each ‘surfacing’ light and brief, and notice how each descent goes further than the last.</p>",

        // ================= Light, infrasound and breathing (special cases)
        "lj_ganzopen" => "<p><b>This one is done with your eyes open:</b> sit about 30 cm from the screen in a dark room and look at the red-and-black flicker, letting your eyes relax.</p>",
        "lj_phone" => "<p>Hold the phone with the flashlight pointing at your closed eyes, about 10 to 20 cm away, or rest it on your chest pointing up at your face.</p>",
        _ => return None,
    })
}
